<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="notes-recurrence" component="recurrence">
  <title>Recurrence Equations</title>
  <handout xml:id="notes-recurrence-intro">
    <title>Introduction to Advancement Operators</title>
    <page>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Rabbits</title>
          
          
          <p>
            <m>f_{n}= f_{n-1}+f_{n-2}</m>, <m>f_{1}=1</m>, <m>f_{2}=1</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <title>Strings</title>
          
          
          <p>
            <m>t_{n+2}= 3t_{n+1}-t_{n}</m>, <m>t_{1}=3</m>, <m>t_{2}=8</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <title>Regions</title>
          
          
          <p>
            <m>r_{n+1}= r_{n} + (n+1)</m>, <m>r_{1}=2</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
      <definition xml:id="def-linear-recurrence">
        <statement>
          <p workspace="1cm">
            A <term>linear recurrence equation</term> is of the form
            <me>
              c_{0}(n)a_{n+k}+ c_{1}(n)a_{n+k-1}+ c_{2}(n)a_{n+k-2}+ \dots+c_{k}(n)a_{n}= g(n),
            </me>
            where <m>k\ge1</m> is an integer and <m>g\colon\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{R}</m> is a function.
          </p>
          <p component="a">
            We call the <m>a_i</m> terms the <term>recursive terms</term> and <term>linear</term> relates to not being able to multiply recursive terms. The functions <m>c_k(n)</m> are the <term>coefficients</term>.
          </p>
          
          <p workspace="1cm">
            If each <m>c_{0}(n),c_{1}(n),\dots,c_{k}(n)</m> is a contant with <m>c_{0}(n),c_{k}(n)\neq0</m>, then we say the equation has <term>constant coefficients</term>.
            <md component="a">
              c_0a_{n+k} + c_1 a_{n+k-1} + c_2 a_{n+k-2} + \cdots + c_k a_n = g(n)
            </md>
            
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>
      <p>
        <alert>Peer instruction question 1.</alert>
      </p>
      <definition xml:id="def-homog-recurr">
        <statement>
          <p workspace="1cm">
            The linear recurrence equation
            <me>
              c_{0}(n)a_{n+k}+ c_{1}(n)a_{n+k-1}+ c_{2}(n)a_{n+k-2}+ \dots+c_{k}(n)a_{n}= g(n),
            </me>
            is called <term>homogeneous</term> if <m>g(n)=0</m> for all <m>n</m>.
          </p>
          <p component="a">
            Key here is to get all of the recursive terms on one the left-hand side and all non-recursive terms on the right-hand side. After doing that, the equation is homogeneous if and only if the right-hand side is <m>0</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>
      <p>
        <alert>Peer instruction question 2.</alert>
      </p>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>An analogy to calculus (or differential equations)</title>
        <p workspace="5cm">
          Let's write <m>D</m> for the differential operator <m>d/dx</m>. Solve the equation
          <me>
            Df = 5f
          </me>
          where <m>f</m> is a differentiable function of <m>x</m> with <m>f(0)=3</m> and 
        </p>
      
        <p component="a">
          Put another way: Find a function <m>f</m> such that <m>f'(x) = 5f(x)</m> and <m>f(0)=3</m>. The function <m>f(x) = 3e^{5x}</m> has this property because <m>D[3e^{5x}] = 5\cdot 3\cdot e^{5x}</m> and <m>f(0)=3</m>.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </page>
    <page>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>f\colon \mathbb{Z}\to \mathbb{R}</m>.
              The <term>advancement operator</term> <m>A</m> is defined so that <m>Af(n) = f(n+1)</m> for all <m>n\in \mathbb{Z}</m>.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
              Here we think of sequences as functions. Usually we think of sequences as only being infinite in one direction, but it will be convenient here to allow them to be infinite in both directions. We can extend to <m>-\infty</m> by filling with <m>0</m>. I usually read <m>Af(n)</m> as <q><m>A</m> of <m>f(n)</m></q>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p workspace="1cm">
              For <m>p</m> a positive integer, <m>A^{p}f(n)</m> denotes applying <m>A</m> to <m>f(n)</m> <m>p</m> times.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
              <m>A^2f(n) = AAf(n) = Af(n+1)=f(n+2)</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <activity workspace="1cm">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Rewrite each of the following expressions so that it does not use the advancement operator.
            <ol marker="a." cols="4">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>A^2f(4)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>A^2f(n)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>A^7f(n)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>A^pf(n)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            <ol marker="a." cols="4">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(6)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(n+2)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(n+7)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f(n+p)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
      <activity>
        <task workspace="1cm">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Let <m>f(n) = 3n+4</m>. Verify that <m>(A^{3}-2A^{2}+7A+2)f(2) = 98</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution>
            <p>
              <me>
                f(5)-2f(4)+7f(3)+2f(2) = 98
              </me>
            </p>
          </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="10cm">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Write each of the following recurrence equations as advancement operator equations.
              <ol cols="3" marker="i.">
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>t_{n+2}= 3t_{n+1}-t_{n}</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
                
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>r_{n+1}= r_{n} + (n+1)</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
                
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>f_{n}= f_{n-1}+f_{n-2}</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution>
            <p>
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>(A^2-3A+1)t(n)=0</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>(A-1)r(n)=n+1</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>(A^2-A-1)f(n)=0</m>
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </solution>
        </task>
      </activity>
    </page>
  </handout>
  <handout>
    <title>Homogeneous Recurrence Equations</title>
    <example>
      <title>Solving Advancement Operator Equations</title>
      
      
      <statement>
        <p workspace="5cm">
          Solve the advancement operator equation
          <me>
            (A+4)f=0
          </me>
          with the initial condition <m>f(0)=2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="a">
        <p>
          One way to examine this is to multiply out the equation, which gives us <m>Af+4f =0</m>, which is equivalent to <m>f(n+1)+4f(n)=0</m> or <m>f(n+1)=-4f(n)</m>. This suggests that we will want to use powers of <m>-4</m>, so we're looking at <m>f(n)=c\cdot(-4)^n</m>. Since <m>f(0)=2</m>, we must have <m>c=2</m> and the solution is <m>f(n) = 2\cdot(-4)^n</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <example>
      <title>Applying Advancement Operator Polynomials</title>
      <statement>
        <p workspace="5cm">
          Let <m>c_{1}</m> and <m>c_{2}</m> be constants and define <m>f(n) = c_{1}3^{n} +c_{2}(-4)^{n}</m>.
          Compute <m>(A+4)(A-3)f(n)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="a">
        <p>
          <!-- Consider adding a version where you distribute and commute the factors. -->
          We start with
          <md>
            <mrow>(A-3)f(n) \amp = (A-3)[c_13^n + c_2(-4)^n]</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp= (A-3)[c_1 3^n] + (A-3)[c_2 (-4)^n] </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = c_1 3^{n+1} - 3c_1 3^n + c_2(-4)^{n+1} - 3c_2 (-4)^n</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = -4c_2(-4)^n -3c_2(-4)^n = -7c_2(-4)^n</mrow>
          </md>.
          Now we apply <m>(A+4)</m> to this and have
          <md>
            <mrow>(A+4)[(A-3)[f(n)]] \amp = (A+4)[-7c_2(-4)^n</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = -7c_2(-4)^{n+1} -4\cdot 7c_2(-4)^n</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = 28c_2(-4)^n -28c_2 (-4)^n </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = 0</mrow>
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-adv-op-distinct">
      <statement>
        <p workspace="2cm">
          The solution to the advancement operator equation
          <me>
            (A-b_{1})(A-b_{2})\cdots (A-b_{k})f(n) = 0
          </me>
          where <m>b_{i}\neq b_{j}</m> if <m>i\neq j</m> is
          <md component="a">
            f(n) = c_1b_1^n + c_2b_2^n + \cdots + c_kb_k^n, \qquad c_i\in\mathbb{R}
          </md>.
        </p>
        <p component="a">
          <alert>Notice that this restriction forces the roots to be distinct!!</alert>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      <alert>Note: </alert>
      When solving
        <me>
          p(A)f(n)=g(n)
        </me>
        we'll focus on <m>p(0)\neq 0</m>.
        Any factors of <m>A</m> in <m>p(A)</m> could be dealt with by shifting solutions.
    </p>
  </handout>
  <handout xml:id="notes-recurrence-rubots">
    <title>Solving a Nonlinear Recurrence</title>
    <page>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>RUBOTS</title>
      <p>
        <dl>
          <li>
            <title>Rooted</title>
            <p component="b">
              <nbsp/>
            </p>
            <p component="a">
              One special vertex, drawn at the top
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Unlabeled</title>
            <p component="b">
              <nbsp/>
            </p>
            <p component="a">
              Vertices don't have <q>names</q>, unlike when we counted labeled trees.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Binary</title>
            <p component="b">
              <nbsp/>
            </p>
            <p component="a">
              Every vertex has 0 or 2 children
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Ordered</title>
            <p component="b">
              <nbsp/>
            </p>
            <p component="a">
              Left child and right child so that the tree in which the left child of the root has two children and the right has none is different to the tree in which the left child of the root has no children and the right child has two.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Trees</title>
            <p component="b">
              <nbsp/>
            </p>
            <p component="a">
              Connected, acyclic graphs.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
      <p>
        By convention, we want the <m>1</m>-vertex graph to be a RUBOT with one leaf.
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>c_{n}</m> denote the number of RUBOTs with <m>n</m> <em>leaves</em>.
        (<alert>NOT</alert> vertices!) Take <m>c_{0}=0</m> by convention.
        The generating function for the number of RUBOTs is then <md>C(x)\displaystyle = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} c_{n}x^{n} = \phantom{0+1x+1x^2+2x^3+5x^4 + \cdots}</md>
      </p>
      <activity>
        <statement>
          <p workspace="3cm">
            Find <m>c_{1}</m>, <m>c_{2}</m>, <m>c_{3}</m>, and <m>c_{4}</m> by drawing the RUBOTs with <m>1</m>, <m>2</m>, <m>3</m>, and <m>4</m> leaves and then start writing out <m>C(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>The RUBOTs with at most four leaves</shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
              <diagram dimensions="(600,300)" margins="15">
                <coordinates bbox="(0,-1,11,5)">
                  <network node-fill="#eee">
                    <node at="0" p="(1,5)" />
                    <node at="1" p="(2.5,5)" edges="[2,3]"/>
                    <node at="2" p="(2,4)" />
                    <node at="3" p="(3,4)"/>
                    <node at="4" p="(5,5)" edges="[5,6]"/>
                    <node at="5" p="(4.5,4)" edges="[7,8]" />
                    <node at="6" p="(5.5,4)"/>
                    <node at="7" p="(4,3)" />
                    <node at="8" p="(5,3)"/>

                    <node at="9" p="(8,5)" edges="[10,11]"/>
                    <node at="10" p="(7.5,4)"  />
                    <node at="11" p="(8.5,4)" edges="[12,13]"/>
                    <node at="12" p="(8,3)" />
                    <node at="13" p="(9,3)"/>

                    <node at="14" p="(1.5,2.5)" edges="[15,16]"/>
                    <node at="15" p="(1,1.5)" edges="[17,18]"  />
                    <node at="16" p="(2,1.5)"/>
                    <node at="17" p="(0.5,0.5)" edges="[40,41]"/>
                    <node at="18" p="(1.5,0.5)"/>
                    <node at="40" p="(0,-0.5)"/>
                    <node at="41" p="(1,-0.5)"/>

                    <node at="19" p="(3.5,2.5)" edges="[20,21]"/>
                    <node at="20" p="(3,1.5)" edges="[22,23]" />
                    <node at="21" p="(4,1.5)" />
                    <node at="22" p="(2.5,0.5)" />
                    <node at="23" p="(3.5,0.5)" edges="[42,43]"/>
                    <node at="42" p="(3,-0.5)"/>
                    <node at="43" p="(4,-0.5)"/>

                    <node at="24" p="(5.5,2.5)" edges="[25,26]"/>
                    <node at="25" p="(5,1.5)"  edges="[44,45]"/>
                    <node at="26" p="(6,1.5)" edges="[27,28]"/>
                    <node at="27" p="(5.7,0.5)" />
                    <node at="28" p="(6.5,0.5)"/>
                    <node at="44" p="(4.5,0.5)"/>
                    <node at="45" p="(5.3,0.5)"/>

                    <node at="30" p="(7.5,2.5)" edges="[31,32]"/>
                    <node at="31" p="(7,1.5)" />
                    <node at="32" p="(8,1.5)" edges="[33,34]"  />
                    <node at="33" p="(7.5,0.5)" edges="[35,36]"/>
                    <node at="34" p="(8.5,0.5)"/>
                    <node at="35" p="(7,-0.5)"/>
                    <node at="36" p="(8,-0.5)"/>

                    <node at="50" p="(9.5,2.5)" edges="[51,52]"/>
                    <node at="51" p="(9,1.5)"  />
                    <node at="52" p="(10,1.5)" edges="[53,54]"/>
                    <node at="53" p="(9.5,0.5)" />
                    <node at="54" p="(10.5,0.5)" edges="[55,56]"/>
                    <node at="55" p="(10,-0.5)"/>
                    <node at="56" p="(11,-0.5)"/>
                  </network>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>  
            
          </image>
          <p>
            <m>c_1 = 1</m>, <m>c_2 = 1</m>, <m>c_3 = 2</m>, <m>c_4 = 5</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
      <activity>
        <title>Decomposing RUBOTs</title>
        <task workspace="1cm">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Let <m>l</m> and <m>r</m> be the left and right children, respectively, of the root of a RUBOT. How many RUBOTs with <m>17</m> leaves have <m>12</m> of those leaves descendants of <m>l</m> and <m>5</m> descendants of <m>r</m>? 
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution>
            <p>
              <m>c_5\cdot c_{12}</m>
            </p>
          </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1cm">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Generalize to a formula for the number of RUBOTs with <m>n</m> leaves in which the left child has <m>k</m> leaves.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution>
            <p>
              <m>c_k\cdot c_{n-k}</m>
            </p>
          </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="4cm">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Give a nonlinear recurrence for <m>c_{n}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution>
            <p>
              <md>
                c_n = \sum_{k=1}^{n-1} c_k\cdot c_{n-k}
              </md>
              
            </p>
          </solution>
        </task>
      </activity>
      </paragraphs>
    </page>
    <page>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow> C(x) \amp = 0 + x + c_1c_1 x^2 + (c_1c_2+c_2c_1)x^3 + \cdots +
            \sum_{k=1}^{n-1}c_k c_{n-k} x^n+ \cdots </mrow>
          <mrow> C(x)\amp= 0 + x + (c_0c_2 + c_1c_1 +c_2c_0)x^2 + \cdots + \sum_{k=0}^{n}c_k c_{n-k} x^n+ \cdots </mrow>
          <mrow> C^2(x) \amp = c_0^2 + (c_0c_1 + c_1c_0)x + (c_0c_2 +c_1c_1 + c_2c_0)x^2 + \cdots + \sum_{k=0}^{n}c_k c_{n-k} x^n+ \cdots</mrow>
          <mrow> C(x) \amp= x+C^2(x) </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <activity workspace="2cm">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the quadratic formula to solve
            <me>
              C^{2}(x) -C(x) + x = 0
            </me>
            for <m>C(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="a">
          <p>
            <me>
              C(x) = \frac{1\pm \sqrt{1-4x}}{2}= \frac{1}{2}\pm \frac{1}{2}(1-4x)^{1/2}
            </me>
            We want a constant term of 0, which means that we need to choose the minus from the <m>\pm</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
      <p workspace="10cm">
        Recall that <m>\displaystyle (1-4x)^{-1/2}= \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \binom{2n}{n}x^{n}</m>.
      </p>
      <p component="a">
        <md>
          <mrow>\int(1-4x)^{-1/2}\, dx  \amp= \int -\frac{1}{4} u^{-1/2}\, du  = \frac{1}{2}u^{1/2} + D</mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = -\frac{1}{2}(1-4x)^{1/2} + D</mrow>
          <mrow>\int\sum_{n=0}^\infty \binom{2n}{n}x^n \amp = \sum_{n=0}^\infty \frac{1}{n+1}\binom{2n}{n}x^{n+1} + E</mrow>
        </md>
        We need <m>D=1/2</m> to get exactly <m>C(x)</m>. That then gives us a constant term of 0, so we take <m>E=0</m>.
      </p>
      <theorem>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The generating function for the number <m>c_{n}</m> of rooted, unlabeled, binary, ordered trees with <m>n</m> leaves is
            <me>
              C(x) = \frac{1-\sqrt{1-4x}}{2}= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n}\binom{2n-2}{n-1}x^{n}.
            </me>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>
    </page>
    <page>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Ramsey Numbers</title>
         
         <definition>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The <term>Ramsey number</term> <m>R(m,n)</m> is the least number <m>t</m> such that every graph on at least <m>t</m> vertices contains a clique of size <m>m</m> or an independent set of size <m>n</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </definition>
        <activity>
          <task workspace="2cm">
            <statement>
              <p>
                What is a clique? What is an independent set?
              </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
              <p>
                A clique is a set of vertices, each pair of which are adjacent. An independent set is a set of vertices, no pair of which are adjacent.
              </p>
            </solution>
          </task>
          
          <task workspace="1cm">
            <statement>
              <p>
                Explain why <m>R(2,2) = 2</m>.
              </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
              <p>
                As soon as you have two vertices, they must either have an edge between them or be non-adjacent. The first creates a 2-clique and the second creates an independent set of size 2.
              </p>
            </solution>
          </task>
          
          <task workspace="2cm">
            <statement>
              <p>
                Can you draw a <m>4</m>-vertex graph that has neither a <m>3</m>-clique nor an independent set of size <m>3</m>? What about <m>5</m> vertices? <m>6</m> vertices?
              </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
              <p>
                For four vertices, consider <m>\mathbf{C}_4</m>. For five vertices, consider <m>\mathbf{C}_5</m>. This is impossible for six vertices.
              </p>
            </solution>
          </task>
        </activity>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-r33">
          <statement>
            <p workspace="10cm">
              The Ramsey number <m>R(3,3) =6</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <proof component="a">
            <p>
              Fix a vertex <m>v</m>. The degree of <m>v</m> is an integer between 0 and 5. We first consider the case where the degree of <m>v</m> is at least 3. In this case, if any pair amongst the (at least) three neighbors of <m>v</m> are adjacent, we have a <m>\bfK_{3}</m> formed by <m>v</m> and a pair of its adjacent neighbors. Otherwise, there are no edges amongst these (at least) three neighbors of <m>v</m>, and we have a 3-vertex (or larger) independent set. Now suppose that the degree of <m>v</m> is at most 2. In this case, if any pair amongst the (at least) three non-neighbors of <m>v</m> are <em>not</em> adjacent, then <m>v</m> along with those two vertices forms a 3-vertex independent set. Otherwise, all of the non-neighbors of <m>v</m> are adjacent and form a clique. Since <m>v</m> has at least 3 non-neighbors in this case, we have a clique of size at least 3.
            </p>
          </proof>
        </proposition>
        <p>
          Paul Erdős on <m>R(5,5)</m> vs <m>R(6,6)</m> and <url href="https://www.combinatorics.org/files/Surveys/ds1/ds1v16-2021.pdf"><pubtitle>Small Ramsey Numbers</pubtitle></url> by S.P. Radziszowski
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </page>
  </handout>
</chapter>
